+# BLOCKED: contradictory underscore requirements
+
+`test_slug.py` cannot pass in full. Two tests require mutually exclusive output
+for the *same* input from the *same* function:
+
+```python
+# test_preserves_underscores (comment cites Spec 4.2)
+slugify("user_id field") == "user_id-field"
+
+# test_underscores_become_dashes (comment cites Spec 4.5)
+slugify("user_id field") == "user-id-field"
+```
+
+`slugify` takes only the title and has no mode/flag, so both assertions call it
+identically. It is impossible to return both strings for one call. No
+implementation can satisfy both; the two spec clauses contradict each other on
+underscore handling.
+
+I did not work around this (e.g. call-order-dependent or stateful behavior);
+that would make `slugify` non-deterministic and would not resolve the spec
+conflict.
+
+## Resolution needed
+
+The content team must decide which rule wins:
+
+- **Spec 4.2 / `test_preserves_underscores`**: keep underscores as-is
+  (`user_id-field`), which means the "slugs only contain `[a-z0-9-]`" invariant
+  in Spec 4.5 must be amended.
+- **Spec 4.5 / `test_underscores_become_dashes`**: convert underscores to
+  dashes (`user-id-field`), which means Spec 4.2 must be removed or reworded.
+
+Once one test is removed/corrected, the implementation (currently following
+Spec 4.5) will pass the whole suite.
+
+## Current status
+
+`python3 -m unittest test_slug` runs 7 tests: 6 pass, 1 fails
+(`test_preserves_underscores`) solely because of the contradiction above.
+All non-conflicting behavior (lowercasing, punctuation stripping, dash
+collapsing, accent folding, trimming) is implemented and passing.
